@haircommander: the job is green again, but we don't know why bumping the memory requirement was needed. This issue is about finding that explanation and (depending on that) maybe going back to less memory.

@thc1006: it's possible to compare memory usage in dashboards:

- [ci-node-e2e-containerd-1-7-cos-dra](https://monitoring-gke.prow.k8s.io/d/96Q8oOOZk/builds?orgId=1&from=now-24h&to=now&timezone=browser&var-datasource=prometheus&var-org=kubernetes&var-repo=kubernetes&var-job=ci-node-e2e-containerd-1-7-cos-dra&var-build=$__all&refresh=30s)
- [ci-node-e2e-containerd-1-7-ubuntu-dra](https://monitoring-gke.prow.k8s.io/d/96Q8oOOZk/builds?orgId=1&from=now-24h&to=now&timezone=browser&var-datasource=prometheus&var-org=kubernetes&var-repo=kubernetes&var-job=ci-node-e2e-containerd-1-7-ubuntu-dra&var-build=$__all&refresh=30s)

The much higher memory usage for ubuntu is clearly visible.

Dear @pohly 

Thanks for reopening this. Agreed: test-infra#37491 restored the jobs, but it did not explain the Ubuntu/COS memory difference.

I re-read the boundary runs, the full #140701 discussion, and the current GCE runner path. A few earlier conclusions were too strong:

* the deployed limit was 1Gi, not 6Gi;
* the OOM is in the host-side e2e_node.test process before VM creation, so Ubuntu guest package installation or guest log output cannot be the cause;
* the same Kubernetes test package passed in the COS job, so #140543 is not sufficient to explain the Ubuntu-only failure;
* COS also uses image_family, so this is not simply a floating Ubuntu image versus a pinned COS image.

The strongest host-side candidate is getGCEImage in `test/e2e_node/remote/gce/gce_runner.go`. For every family or regex configuration it currently runs an unrestricted:

```text
gcloud compute images list --format=json --project=<project>
```

The complete stdout is buffered, all image resources are unmarshaled, and filtering and sorting happen afterward in the Go process. Ubuntu uses `ubuntu-os-gke-cloud` and an additional regex, while COS uses `cos-cloud`. A larger project response or higher gcloud processing cost can therefore explain the Ubuntu-only memory delta before a VM exists.

I consider that the leading hypothesis, not yet a completed RCA. I will keep the 2Gi mitigation while measuring, on paired Ubuntu and COS runs:

1. image count and JSON output size;
2. gcloud child MaxRSS separately from e2e_node.test and cgroup peak;
3. gcloud and runner-image versions;
4. the selected image;
5. the current lookup versus field projection, family filtering, and streaming selection.

The permanent change should preserve image-selection semantics. After repeated canaries demonstrate enough headroom, I will test whether the memory can safely return to 1Gi.

I also found a separate correctness bug: `.*[^-cgroupsv1]$` is a negated character class, not “does not end with -cgroupsv1”. I will keep that fix separate because changing it may change the selected image and would confound the memory experiment.

